WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.115
(violin music)

00:00:04.115 --> 00:00:06.230
- [Voiceover] Every age
I turn I think about

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.730
how old of a child my mom
had when she was my age.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.250
At 21 she had a one year old,

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.971
at 25, a five year old.

00:00:14.971 --> 00:00:17.691
I try to imagine what
my life would be like

00:00:17.691 --> 00:00:19.651
now with a child.

00:00:19.651 --> 00:00:22.111
I got glimpses of it carting
around children in the snow

00:00:22.111 --> 00:00:24.071
through Brooklyn when I was a nanny.

00:00:24.071 --> 00:00:25.771
The brand of mom I nannied for

00:00:25.771 --> 00:00:28.491
differed greatly from my own mother.

00:00:28.491 --> 00:00:30.571
These moms were all over 40.

00:00:30.571 --> 00:00:32.471
In fact most were over 50,

00:00:32.471 --> 00:00:36.531
and they had houses and
plans and firm goals.

00:00:36.531 --> 00:00:39.111
These ladies bought organic produce.

00:00:39.111 --> 00:00:41.291
They were nice, don't get me wrong,

00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:44.011
but my mom and I had more fun.

00:00:44.011 --> 00:00:46.811
When I was little I would sit
in the front seat of the car

00:00:46.811 --> 00:00:48.691
and we would belt Alanis Morisette.

00:00:48.691 --> 00:00:51.458
Caps for Sale was Craps for Sale

00:00:51.458 --> 00:00:53.348
every time I convinced her to read

00:00:53.348 --> 00:00:55.385
it that way to me at bedtime.

00:00:55.385 --> 00:00:58.365
And I wouldn't be able to stop laughing.

00:00:58.365 --> 00:00:59.945
When other twenty-somethings

00:00:59.945 --> 00:01:02.245
were bumbling at bars until closing time,

00:01:02.245 --> 00:01:04.886
and washing hookups down with cold pizza,

00:01:04.886 --> 00:01:08.086
my mom was busy being a mom.

00:01:08.086 --> 00:01:10.966
In time though, I did turn into a teenager

00:01:10.966 --> 00:01:14.226
who could wipe their own snot
and make their own meals.

00:01:14.226 --> 00:01:17.006
Moms and teenage daughters
notoriously spark

00:01:17.006 --> 00:01:18.846
misunderstandings and melodrama,

00:01:18.846 --> 00:01:20.746
and we weren't any different.

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:22.625
In arguments I would glare at her

00:01:22.625 --> 00:01:24.605
and hatefully spit the words,

00:01:24.605 --> 00:01:26.886
you're suppose to be a mom.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.226
I was adamant that moms

00:01:28.226 --> 00:01:31.306
didn't litter the kitchen
table with bottles and mixers.

00:01:31.306 --> 00:01:33.226
They didn't date 21 year olds,

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:36.145
and that they didn't
attempt Sylvia Plath antics

00:01:36.145 --> 00:01:38.246
involving ovens.

00:01:38.246 --> 00:01:41.225
I've always hated Sylvia Plath since

00:01:41.225 --> 00:01:44.566
and have refused to
read any word she wrote.

00:01:44.566 --> 00:01:48.486
I forgot that before being
a mom she was a person.

00:01:48.486 --> 00:01:51.446
A person on their own journey.

00:01:51.446 --> 00:01:54.346
A person who experiences sadness,

00:01:54.346 --> 00:01:56.586
and needs help too.

00:01:56.586 --> 00:01:59.046
A person whose identity is not just mom,

00:01:59.046 --> 00:02:02.585
but also woman and artist and lover.

00:02:02.585 --> 00:02:04.046
Sometimes I think about my mother

00:02:04.046 --> 00:02:06.786
like she's a person who
isn't related to me.

00:02:06.786 --> 00:02:10.486
I marvel at how brave
and strong this woman is,

00:02:10.486 --> 00:02:13.826
to have raised me without
the support of her family.

00:02:13.826 --> 00:02:15.846
This woman who put herself through college

00:02:15.846 --> 00:02:17.946
by working at minimum wage jobs

00:02:17.946 --> 00:02:20.506
while still taking care of me.

00:02:20.506 --> 00:02:22.586
And I can only feel grateful,

00:02:22.586 --> 00:02:26.486
grateful that I have a
mom whose not just a mom,

00:02:26.486 --> 00:02:28.784
but is also a friend.

00:02:28.784 --> 00:02:33.784
(violin music)

